<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#doge-of-venice">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doge of Venice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">697–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Doge, Dux Venetiarum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The doge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the highest authority of the Republic of Venice (697–1797), head of state and of the Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, elected for life. The title comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dux, leader, and grew out of the offices of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>; the first historically attested doge was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orso-ipato">Orso Ipato</a> in the first half of the 8th century. From 1268 the doge was chosen by a complex machinery of lots and nominating committees in the Great Council, meant to limit the influence of the great families, and he was kept under strict surveillance. As the oligarchy took over his functions the office became mostly representative. The last doge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a>, abdicated in 1797 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice.jpg" alt="Doge of Venice" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardino Castelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodovico_Manin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word doge comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dux, leader, and the office grew out of older offices in the late Roman and early Byzantine empires. In the 6th and 7th centuries the Byzantine province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> shrank to coastal lagoons and was governed by a magister militum subordinate to the exarch of Ravenna, with the seat moving from Oderzo to Eraclea, then Malamocco and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a> in the 9th century. The first historically attested doge was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orso-ipato">Orso Ipato</a>; accounts of earlier doges by the chronicler John the Deacon, around the start of the 11th century, are not considered reliable by modern scholars. Early doges bore Byzantine titles, and as late as 1202 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a> was styled protosebastos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The doge's powers were never precisely defined. Several doges associated a son with themselves in office, until a law forbade any doge to associate a family member or name a successor. After 1172 a committee of forty chose the doge, and the regulations of 1268, in force until 1797, sought to limit great families through a machinery in which members of the Great Council were reduced by lot and by election through successive stages until forty-one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> chose the doge, who needed at least twenty-five votes. After 1268 the doge was under strict surveillance: he had to wait for other officials before opening dispatches and could own no property abroad.</p><p>After his death a commission of inquisitori judged his acts, and his estate could be fined. The title changed with Venetian conquests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-ii-orseolo">Pietro II Orseolo</a> adopted dux Dalmatiae after taking Dalmatian territory in 1000, and by the early 12th century the title included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> the doge was styled lord of a fourth part and a half of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zadar">Treaty of Zadar</a> of 1358 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, and the title was reduced to Duke of the Venices and the rest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The doge had a ritual role. On Ascension Day he cast a ring from the state barge, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucentaur">Bucentaur</a>, into the Adriatic in the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> with the sea, a ceremony first instituted for Orseolo's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in 1000. From the 14th century his symbol was the horn-shaped ducal cap, the corno ducale. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> developed, the more important functions passed to other officials and boards, and the office became mostly representative. The last doge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a>, abdicated in 1797 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s conquest, and Venice never revived the title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Doge of Venice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
